{0 Building the layer from scratch}

The second of two pages about how this engine is made. The first,
{{!page-"building-the-engine"} Building the engine from scratch}, rebuilds the
raycaster: rays against wall segments, a floor cast per pixel, portals drawn
recursively into an opening. It ends with a renderer you can point at a
{!Camlcast_core.World} and a loop you can drive one with.

This page is about what sits over that. A game does not build a
{!Camlcast_core.World}; it {e describes} one, every frame, from nothing, and
something works out what changed. That something is about four hundred lines,
none of it knows what a wall is, and this page rebuilds it in the order the
problems arrive.

If you want to {e use} the layer rather than build it,
{{!page-"making-a-game"} Making a game on CamlCast} is the page you want.

{1 The problem, stated once}

A raycaster wants a [World]: an array of rooms, each an array of walls, joined
by portals carrying transforms. Building one is not cheap to write but it is
cheap to run — twenty microseconds for a hundred and forty-five walls, against
fourteen milliseconds to draw that same frame.

A game wants something else entirely. It wants to say {e a torch is lit}, and
have a torch that is lit. It wants a door that knows whether it is open, an
enemy that knows where it is going, a fuse that knows how much is left — each
of those written next to the thing it belongs to, not in one function that
advances all of them together.

The old shape of a game on this engine was the second thing forced into the
first:

{[
  type t = { phase : phase; left : float; player : Player.t }

  let update state ~dt ~motion ~actions = (* everything, at once *)
  let view state = (* the whole world, from scratch *)
]}

Every moving part of a world lives in one record and one function, because that
is the only place there is to put them. There is no entity system —
{!Camlcast_core.Room.val-sprite} is an inert record with no identity and no
behaviour — so a torch that flickers, a door that opens and an enemy that walks
are all branches of the same [update].

React solved this for documents, and the solution is not the virtual DOM. It is
this: {b let the program describe what should exist, and keep the state
somewhere the description does not have to mention.} The description is thrown
away every frame. What survives is a tree beside it, and the whole trick is
matching the two up.

{1 Phase 1 — What a description is}

Start with the type. A description is a tree, and it has four shapes:

{[
  type 'prim t =
    | Empty
    | Fragment of 'prim t list
    | Prim of { prim : 'prim; key : string option; children : 'prim t list }
    | Component : {
        render : 'props -> 'prim t;
        props : 'props;
        key : string option;
        name : string;
      }
        -> 'prim t
]}

Three things to notice.

{b It is parameterised over ['prim].} A description does not know what a wall
is. That is what lets the reconciler be tested against a host made of strings,
with no renderer linked in at all — which is how the hard part of this engine is
checked as text rather than as a picture.

{b [Component] is a GADT constructor.} ['props] appears only inside it, so it is
existentially quantified: a parent can hold children of many different prop types
in one list, and only the component itself ever sees its own. Without this the
type would have to be parameterised over every prop type in the tree, which is
another way of saying it would not work.

{b Nothing is called yet.} [Component] holds a function and its argument. The
description of a subtree is built lazily, one level at a time, as something walks
into it.

{2 What identifies a component}

Two descriptions at the same place are the same component when their [render]
functions are {e physically the same closure}. Nothing else would do: a function
is the only thing a component is.

That has a consequence a user has to live with, and it is worth being blunt about
it. A component built inside another render is a fresh closure every frame, is
therefore never the same component as last frame, and is torn down and rebuilt —
along with everything under it — sixty times a second. Its state will appear not
to work.

{[
  let declare ~name render ?key props = Component { render; props; key; name }
]}

[declare] exists to make the right thing the easy thing: the closure it captures
is made once, when the module is initialised.

{1 Phase 2 — The tree that survives}

Beside the description, an instance tree with the same shape:

{[
  type instance =
    | Nothing
    | Fragment of { path : Path.t; children : instance list }
    | Primitive of { path : Path.t; prim : H.prim; key : string option;
                     children : instance list }
    | Component of { path : Path.t; render_id : Obj.t; key : string option;
                     name : string; slots : Hook.slots; child : instance }
]}

Reconciling walks the two together. At each place it asks: is the thing described
the thing already here? Same component (same closure, same key) or same primitive
(same key) means keep the instance and walk into it. Anything else means unmount
the old subtree deepest-first and mount the new one.

{2 The one [Obj] you cannot avoid, and the one you can}

[render_id] is an [Obj.t]. Two components' [render] functions have different
types whenever their props differ, so [==] cannot be applied to them directly —
it would not typecheck. [Obj.repr] erases the type and nothing else, and physical
equality on the result asks exactly the question meant: are these the same
closure? Nothing is ever cast back, so nothing is assumed about what is behind
the pointer. This is the safe kind.

The unsafe kind arrives in Phase 4.

{2 Keys}

Among one parent's children, a keyed description is matched to last frame's by
its key wherever it has moved to, and an unkeyed one by its position. That is
React's rule, for React's reason: a list of enemies that sorts itself by distance
keeps each enemy's state only if the enemies are keyed.

The implementation is worth one paragraph because a detail in it matters. Old
children go into an array; keyed ones are indexed by key; claiming one empties
its slot rather than removing it. That last part is what keeps the {e leftovers}
— the ones nothing claimed, which are about to be unmounted — in the order they
were declared. A hashtable walked at the end would unmount them in whatever
order it felt like, and a trace of a frame would not read the same way twice.

{2 Say what you did}

Reconciling is the part of this engine that is hardest to be sure of and easiest
to get subtly wrong: a component torn down when it should have been kept still
looks right on screen, and only shows itself later as state that will not
persist.

So the reconciler says what it did — mounted, updated, unmounted, with a path —
and the suite asserts on that:

{v
update   #0 : room
update   #0/torch[two]
update   #0/torch[two]/#0 : flame
update   #0/torch[one]
update   #0/torch[one]/#0 : flame
v}

That is a list of two torches written in the other order, with nothing remounted.
It is not a claim you can check by looking at a picture.

{1 Phase 3 — Paths}

A path is the chain of steps from the root to a node, and it is two things at
once.

It is an {b identity}: keyed steps by key, unkeyed steps by index, names ignored
throughout. That is what the reconciler matches on and what everything hanging
off an instance is found by.

It is also a {b label}: when a level fails a check, the report has to say which
part of a game wrote the offending geometry. [plaza / gallery_wall] is an answer
someone can act on; [World.make: threshold linked twice: plaza.east] is not.

So it prints two ways. The friendly one drops anonymous steps, because a wall
being the fourth child of its room helps nobody. The debug one keeps them, because
a trace has to tell two sibling walls apart. Two spellings of the same value, for
two different readers.

{1 Phase 4 — Hooks, on effect handlers}

A component is a function called afresh every frame, so anything it wants to
remember has to live somewhere else. That somewhere is a row of slots on its
instance, and a hook is a request for the next one.

The interesting question is how a plain function reaches them. OCaml 5 answers
it:

{[
  type _ Effect.t +=
    | Use_state : 'a -> ('a * ('a -> unit)) Effect.t
    | Use_ref : 'a -> 'a ref Effect.t
    | Use_memo : { compute : unit -> 'a; deps : 'd; equal : 'd -> 'd -> bool }
        -> 'a Effect.t
    | Use_effect : { start : unit -> (unit -> unit) option; deps : 'd;
                     equal : 'd -> 'd -> bool }
        -> unit Effect.t
]}

[use_state] performs an effect. The one place a component's function is called
installs a handler for it. That is the whole mechanism, and it is why a game
writes

{[
  let lit, set_lit = Hook.use_state true in
]}

in an ordinary function with an ordinary type, with nothing about the plumbing
appearing in the signature. A state monad would have put it in the signature; a
context argument would have put it in every call site.

{2 The slot table, and the cast}

A row of slots is heterogeneous, and OCaml has no other way to say so than
[Obj.t]. The cast back is sound exactly when the hook rule holds: the same call
site reaches the same slot every time, so the type it stored is the type it
reads.

What makes that more than a hope is a tag on each cell. Three of the four ways to
break the rule become an exception rather than a bad cast:

- a [use_ref] where a [use_state] used to be — the tags differ;
- a render asking for more hooks than last time — the row has settled;
- a render stopping early and asking for fewer — the cursor did not reach the
  end.

The fourth gets through: a conditional swapping one [use_state] for another
[use_state] of a {e different type} at the same index. Same kind, same slot,
different type. That is unsound rather than merely wrong, and it is written down
as such in {!Camlcast.Hook} rather than glossed. Do not write conditional hooks.

{2 When a setter takes effect}

React re-renders until the tree settles. This does not: a setter writes its slot,
marks the root, and the change shows up on the next frame.

That is a trade, and worth stating as one. It costs sixteen milliseconds of
latency at sixty frames a second, which nobody has noticed. It buys the
impossibility of a render loop that does not converge, which is a class of bug
this engine simply does not have. A game renders every frame anyway; a document
does not, which is why React cannot make the same trade.

{2 Effects run after the frame}

[use_effect]'s work is queued, not run. It happens after the scene is assembled,
with every cleanup before any setup — so a component leaving and a component
arriving in the same frame never overlap on whatever they both hold. Unmounting
queues its cleanups into that same flush rather than running them where it
stands, which is what makes the ordering hold across a teardown as well as an
update.

Two things follow from queueing, and both had to be written down as functions
before they were true.

A frame that is never assembled owes nothing. [assemble] is the last thing that
can refuse a description — the host reads a forest it did not build and may find
it is not a world — and until it has returned there is nothing to commit. So the
tree is put in place after the scene comes back, not before, and a render that
raises throws its queued work away instead of leaving it for the next frame to
flush. Otherwise an effect belonging to a description that was rejected starts
later, under a component that never existed.

What that does not buy is a frame nobody can tell happened. The components ran:
a ref one of them wrote is written, a memo it recomputed is recomputed, and the
trace has already said as much. Rolling those back would mean handing every
component a copy of its own slots and swapping the copies in at the end — and a
[use_ref] hands out a box by identity, so the one thing a component is most
likely to have written is exactly the thing a copy cannot take back. The tree
and the effects are what the runtime owns, and they are what it rolls back. The
rest belongs to the component, and the interface says so rather than promising a
transaction it cannot keep.

A root that is let go of owes everything. Every cleanup this runtime runs is
reached through a later render that removed the component, so a root simply
dropped — a one-shot [Mount.build], a checker's forest, a run whose window has
closed — runs none of them at all, and whatever its effects took stays taken.
{!Camlcast.Mount.destroy} is the missing half of [create]: it unmounts the tree
deepest-first into the same flush, which is the same ordering seen from the end
of a mount's life rather than the middle of one.

{1 Phase 5 — Contexts, and a type witness}

Some things every part of a world needs and no part wants as a prop: the air it
is lit by, the key bindings in force, the font a label is drawn in.

{[
  type 'a t = { id : 'a Type.Id.t; default : 'a }
  type binding = Binding : 'a t * 'a -> binding

  let rec find : type a. binding list -> a t -> a option =
   fun bindings wanted ->
    match bindings with
    | [] -> None
    | Binding (context, value) :: outer -> (
        match Type.Id.provably_equal context.id wanted.id with
        | Some Type.Equal -> Some value
        | None -> find outer wanted)
]}

This is where OCaml does better than the thing it is modelled on, and it is worth
dwelling on for one paragraph. [Type.Id.provably_equal] returns a {e proof}, not
a boolean. Matching on that proof is what lets [value] leave the function at the
type [wanted] was declared with. There is no cast here — and without the witness
the function could not be written at all.

The contrast with Phase 4 is the useful part. A context is a value the game
creates explicitly, so there is somewhere to hang a witness. A hook has only its
position in a row, and there is no per-call-site value to attach anything to.
Where a witness can be had, it is had; where it cannot, the reason is written
down.

{1 Phase 6 — The seam}

Everything so far knows nothing about walls. The whole of what it needs from a
host is this:

{[
  type 'prim node = { path : Path.t; prim : 'prim; children : 'prim node list }

  module type HOST = sig
    type prim
    type scene
    val assemble : prim node list -> scene
  end
]}

Two types and one function. React's host config is written the other way round —
[createInstance], [appendChild], [removeChild] — because the DOM is a mutable
tree that must be poked node by node. The world a raycaster draws is not: it is
an immutable value assembled whole, and the primitives that go into it are cheap
records. Handing over the finished forest and asking for a scene is both simpler
and a better fit.

It is also what makes the reconciler testable. A mock host is a dozen lines — a
primitive is a string, a scene is an indented tree — and every decision the
reconciler makes becomes readable text. And it pays a second time: pointing a
second host at the same description, one that assembles {e nothing} and hands
back the forest, is how the level checker reads a description's structure
without building anything.

{1 Phase 7 — The host for a raycaster}

Now the walls. A primitive is a description of one:

{[
  type t =
    | World of { atmosphere : Atmosphere.t; spawn : string * Vec.t }
    | Room of { name : string; floor : Room.surface; ceiling : Room.ceiling }
    | Wall of { a : Vec.t; b : Vec.t; height : float; material : Material.t;
                reacts : reacts }
    | Decal of Room.decal
    | Threshold of Room.threshold * reacts
    | Sprite of Room.sprite * reacts
    | Camera of camera
    | Cursor
    | Finish
    | Link of { here : string * string; there : string * string }
    | Hud | Rect of ... | Bar of ... | Text of ... | Highlight of ... | ...
]}

A [Wall] carries its endpoints rather than an assembled {!Camlcast_core.Room.val-wall},
because the decals hung on it arrive as its children and have to be in hand
before [Room.wall] can be called at all. Where nothing nests, the assembled value
is carried directly.

[assemble] folds the forest: rooms collect their walls, thresholds and sprites;
the world collects its rooms and links; and out comes a
{!Camlcast_core.World.t}, built by the same [Room.make] and [World.make] a
hand-written level always used.

{2 Two things that are made impossible rather than checked}

{b Winding.} A room is black from the inside if its boundary is wound the wrong
way round. Nothing catches it, nothing wants the reversed version, and the
symptom points nowhere near the cause. So {!Camlcast.P.boundary} measures the
signed area of the loop it was given and winds it correctly: the same corners in
either order build the same room. The measurement is calibrated against
{!Camlcast_core.Room.rectangle}, which the engine documents as impossible to get
wrong, so the definition cannot drift from what defines it.

{b Names, not indices.} The player carries a room index across frames, and a
description that grows a room, loses one, or writes its rooms in another order
makes that index mean something else. So the loop keeps the room's {e name},
looks it up in each new world, and carries the pose across with an identity
transform. Names are what a description says; indices are what assembling one
produces.

{1 Phase 8 — What it costs}

Assembling a whole world every frame sounds like something to design around. It
is not, and the point of measuring was to find out rather than to assume:

{v
describe five rooms         20 us
draw five rooms         14,088 us
v}

Five rooms and a hundred and forty-five walls is the largest world this engine
has. The layer is a seventh of one percent of the frame it is for.

So there is no memoisation in it. No props bailout, no cached room per instance,
none of the machinery a reconciler is expected to carry — because a benchmark
was written before any of it was, and said not to. Every node still carries a
stable path, so caching remains a change to one module if that ever stops being
true, and [bench/frame.exe] is there to be re-run rather than the argument being
had again.

The other number in that table is the interesting one. Drawing five rooms takes
most of a sixty-frame budget on its own, because {!Camlcast_core.Ray.cast}
intersects every wall segment in the room once per screen column and there is no
spatial index. That is the engine as it has always been, has nothing to do with
any layer over it, and is where work would actually pay.

{1 What to read in the source}

In dependency order, and none of them is long:

- [loom/path.ml] — identity and label in one value.
- [loom/element.ml] — the description type, and [declare].
- [loom/trace.ml] — what the reconciler says it did.
- [loom/hook.ml] — the effects, the slot table, the tag check.
- [loom/context.ml] — [Type.Id], and a proof instead of a cast.
- [loom/store.ml] — a reducer and a subscription, and nothing else.
- [loom/reconcile.ml] — the whole of the matching, in one functor.
- [loom/host.ml] — two types and one function.
- [lib/prim.ml], [lib/host.ml] — the walls.

[loom/] depends on nothing but the standard library, and its dune file argues
the case at some length. That is not fastidiousness: it is what makes the mock
host possible, and the mock host is what makes any of this checkable.
